extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Scatter"
	- pagedescription = "Scatter and bubble charts in Tablechart: two numbers per row, as a marker with its name, on two axes."
	- const varieties = [['Elstar', '62', '1.20', '420', 'Eating'], ['Jonagold', '75', '1.05', '310', 'Eating'], ['Braeburn', '48', '1.45', '180', 'Eating'], ['Gala', '55', '1.30', '260', 'Eating'], ['Cox', '35', '1.85', '120', 'Eating'], ['Discovery', '30', '1.75', '60', 'Eating'], ['Boskoop', '70', '0.95', '200', 'Cooking'], ['Granny Smith', '40', '1.60', '90', 'Cooking'], ['Bramley', '70', '0.95', '140', 'Cooking']]

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Scatter
				p.lead Two numbers per row, as a marker with its name.

	+section("Scatter chart")
		p Each row is a marker. The first value column sets where the marker is across, the second where it is up. The headers of those columns are the titles of the axes. The name of the row is shown next to its marker.
		p The scatter chart is an extra: load it before the charts are made. See #[a(href="#load-it") Load it].

		+chart(null, '1,5')
			figure(data-chart="scatter")
				figcaption: b Harvest and price per variety
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p The axes start a little below the lowest value and end a little above the highest, so they do not have to start at zero. The numbers along the axes get the format of the numbers in the table, with the same text around them, such as #[code %].
		p A row without a number in one of the two columns has no marker. It stays in the table.

	+section("Bubbles")
		p A third value column sets the size of each marker. The area of a marker follows its value. The largest marker is #[code --tablechart-bubble-max] across, #[code 24px] by default. The header of the column is shown in the legend, with a small and a large circle.

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="scatter")
				figcaption: b Harvest, price and number of trees
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
							th Trees
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

		p Larger markers come first, so a small marker is on top of a large one.

	+section("Groups")
		p Add #[code data-group] to the header of a column with names, such as "Eating" and "Cooking". Each group gets its own colour and its own shape: a circle, a triangle, a square and a diamond. The groups are in the legend. The colours are #[code --tablechart-color-1], #[code --tablechart-color-2] and so on. See #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) Colours].

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure.scatter-colours(data-chart="scatter")
				figcaption: b Eating and cooking apples
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
							th Trees
							th(data-group) Use
					tbody
						each row in varieties
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]
								td= row[4]

		+style.
			.scatter-colours {
				--tablechart-color-1: #3f7fbf;
				--tablechart-color-2: #d9844f;
			}

		p The group names are cells of the table, so screen readers read them, and with #[code data-chart-keys] they get translation keys, such as #[code apples-group1]. See #[a(href=url('/features/translation.html')) Translation].

	+section("Names")
		p Each name goes to the right of its marker if there is room. If not, it tries the left, above, below, and then the corners. A name does not cover a marker or another name, and stays inside the axes. The larger markers get their place first.
		p If a name has no room at all, it is left out. Then the chart has a tooltip: pointing at a marker shows its name. See #[a(href=url('/features/tooltips.html')) Tooltips].
		p Rows with the same two numbers have their markers in the same place. They share one name, with the number of the others: #[strong Boskoop +1] above. The tooltip of that marker shows all their names.
		p #[code data-chart-names="false"] turns the names off, for every marker. Then the tooltip shows the name of the marker that is pointed at. The #[code names] option does the same for every scatter chart.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<figure data-chart="scatter" data-chart-names="false">

		p Above 400 markers, a chart does not look for room for the names: they are all left out, and the tooltip shows them.

	+section("Axes")
		p #[code data-chart-scale] sets the axis up: two numbers are where it starts and ends, and three or more are the numbers along it. #[code data-chart-scale-x] does the same for the axis across. #[code data-chart-scale-y] is another name for #[code data-chart-scale].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-scale-x="0 100" data-chart-scale="0 0.5 1 1.5 2")
				figcaption: b From zero
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p Without numbers of your own, Tablechart chooses steps of 1, 2 or 5 times 10, 100 and so on. On a narrow chart it shows fewer numbers, so they do not touch.

	+section("Gridlines")
		p #[code data-chart-grid] adds a light line at each number along both axes. The #[code grid] option does the same for every chart with axes.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				figcaption: b With gridlines
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
					tbody
						each row in varieties.slice(0, 6)
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p #[code --tablechart-grid-color], #[code --tablechart-grid-opacity] and #[code --tablechart-grid-width] set how the gridlines look. #[code --tablechart-axis-color], #[code --tablechart-axis-width] and #[code --tablechart-axis-size] set the axes and the text along them. See #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

	+section("Load it")
		p The scatter chart is not in the main script, as most pages do not need it. It is in the extras. See #[a(href=url('/types/extras.html')) Extras].

		+codeblock('JS').
			import { addType, init } from "@martinomagnifico/tablechart";
			import { scatter } from "@martinomagnifico/tablechart/extras";

			addType("scatter", scatter);
			init();

		p With a script tag, load #[code tablechart-extras.js] after #[code tablechart.js]. It adds the scatter chart by itself.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<script src="tablechart.js"></script>
			<script src="tablechart-extras.js"></script>
			<script>
				Tablechart.init();
			</script>

	+section("What it does not do")
		p A scatter chart has no steps, no stacks and no break in the scale. If a figure asks for one, the chart is shown whole. For a stack or a break the console shows a warning; for steps it shows a message with the #[code debug] option.

	+section("Next")
		p To set the colours of the groups, see #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) Colours]. For all the attributes, see #[a(href=url('/reference/attributes.html')) Attributes].
